call_details.response:
The centre column (3) is already “spoiled” for us – the opponent’s o on row 4 breaks any vertical-four there.  
The best strategy in the opening, once you have claimed the bottom centre, is to take one of the two neighbouring columns.  
Dropping in column 2

• puts a second x next to the one we own on the bottom row, creating an immediate two-in-a-row foundation that can grow horizontally or diagonally;  
• keeps the move in the most valuable part of the board (close to the centre);  
• avoids helping the opponent, who gains nothing special from any reply – no column yields an instant threat, and any vertical attempt in the centre can be blocked later;  
• is symmetric to column 4, so either side neighbour is fine, and column 2 is just as strong while leaving us the option of column 4 next.

Other choices are weaker: playing again in column 3 gives no direct threat (the o still breaks the column) and outer columns give away central control.

Hence the optimal move is to drop in column 2.

Final Answer: 2

